## Requirements

Install these on the device that will host MyCloud:

- Docker Desktop on Windows, or Docker Engine with Docker Compose on Linux/Raspberry Pi
- Syncthing
- Git
- Optional: Tailscale for secure access away from home

> The Docker setup steps below are written and tested for Windows with Docker Desktop.

## Setup

### 1. Clone the repository

```powershell
git clone [https://github.com/vaishantn/iCloud_alternative.git](https://github.com/vaishantn/iCloud_alternative.git)
cd iCloud_alternative
```

### 2. Set up Syncthing

1. Install and open Syncthing.
2. Create or choose the Syncthing folder that MyCloud should manage.
3. Open the Syncthing interface in your browser:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:8384
```

4. In Syncthing, go to:

```text
Actions -> Settings -> GUI
```

5. Copy the Syncthing API key.
6. Copy the Windows path of the Syncthing folder.

Example:

```text
C:\Users\YourWindowsUser\Syncthing_save_folder
```

### 3. Create private configuration

Copy the example configuration file:

```powershell
Copy-Item .env_example .env
```

Open the new configuration file:

```powershell
notepad .env
```

Fill in your own values:

```env
FLASK_SECRET_KEY=replace-with-a-long-random-secret
APP_PASSWORD=replace-with-a-strong-password
SYNCTHING_API_KEY=replace-with-your-syncthing-api-key

SYNCTHING_URL=http://host.docker.internal:8384

# Leave this unchanged.
SYNCED_FOLDER_PATH=/data/syncthing

# Change this to your own Syncthing folder.
# Use forward slashes on Windows.
HOST_SYNCED_FOLDER_PATH=C:/Users/YourWindowsUser/Syncthing_save_folder

APP_PORT=5000
DEMO_MODE=false
```

Generate a secure Flask secret key:

```powershell
python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"
```

Copy the generated value into:

```env
FLASK_SECRET_KEY=your-generated-value
```

Do not commit `.env`. It contains private configuration values, including your password, secret key, API key, and local folder path.

### 4. Start MyCloud

Make sure Docker Desktop is running.

From the project folder, run:

```powershell
docker compose up -d --build
```

Open MyCloud:

```text
http://localhost:5000
```

Log in with the password you set for `APP_PASSWORD`.

### 5. Test MyCloud

1. Confirm the dashboard shows files in your Syncthing folder.
2. Upload a small test file.
3. Confirm it appears in your Windows Syncthing folder.
4. Confirm it syncs to other devices connected through Syncthing.

## Daily Commands

### Start MyCloud

```powershell
docker compose up -d
```

### Stop MyCloud

```powershell
docker compose down
```

### Rebuild after code changes

```powershell
docker compose up -d --build
```

### Check container status

```powershell
docker compose ps
```

### View logs

```powershell
docker compose logs --tail=100
```

## Access Away From Home

MyCloud can be used locally on the computer running Docker:

```text
http://localhost:5000
```

For secure access from another network, use Tailscale.

Tailscale creates a private encrypted network between devices you approve. With Tailscale Serve, MyCloud can be reached through a private HTTPS address by devices in your Tailscale network, without opening a router port or making the dashboard publicly available.

### 1. Install Tailscale

Install Tailscale on:

- The computer running Docker and MyCloud
- Every phone, tablet, laptop, or other device that should access MyCloud

Download Tailscale from [tailscale.com/download](https://tailscale.com/download).

On Windows:

1. Download and install Tailscale.
2. Open Tailscale from the system tray.
3. Select **Log in**.
4. Sign in to your Tailscale account.

On your phone or another computer:

1. Install the Tailscale app.
2. Sign in with the same Tailscale account.

### 2. Connect the host computer

On the computer running MyCloud, open PowerShell and run:

```powershell
tailscale up
```

Complete the sign-in flow in your browser, then check:

```powershell
tailscale status
```

### 3. Share MyCloud over Tailscale

Make sure MyCloud is running first:

```powershell
docker compose up -d
```

Then run:

```powershell
tailscale serve localhost:5000
```

Check the Tailscale Serve configuration:

```powershell
tailscale serve status
```

Tailscale will provide an HTTPS address similar to:

```text
[https://your-computer-name.your-tailnet.ts.net](https://your-computer-name.your-tailnet.ts.net)
```

Open that address from another device that is signed in to your Tailscale network.

### Stop Tailscale access

```powershell
tailscale serve off
```

### Tailscale Troubleshooting

#### `tailscale serve status` says `No serve config`

Run:

```powershell
tailscale serve localhost:5000
tailscale serve status
```

#### The Tailscale HTTPS URL does not load

Check that:

- MyCloud is still running:

```powershell
docker compose ps
```

- Tailscale is connected on the computer hosting MyCloud:

```powershell
tailscale status
```

- Tailscale is installed and connected on the remote device.
- The remote device is signed in to the same authorized Tailscale account.
- `tailscale serve status` points to `localhost:5000`.

## Supported Conversions

| Source | Target | Docker support |
|---|---|---|
| PDF | DOCX | Supported |
| JPG, JPEG, PNG, WebP | JPG, PNG, WebP | Supported |
| MP3, WAV, MP4, MKV, MOV, AVI, M4A | MP3, WAV, MP4, MKV | Supported when the source format is supported |
| DOCX | PDF | Not supported in Docker |

## Limitations

- DOCX-to-PDF conversion requires Microsoft Word and Windows-specific automation, so it is not available inside the Linux Docker container.
- Syncthing must be installed and configured separately.
- MyCloud must be running for the dashboard to be available.
- MyCloud is designed for personal or trusted-device use, not public internet hosting.
- The Docker setup instructions are written and tested for Windows with Docker Desktop. Linux and Raspberry Pi hosts may need platform-specific Docker and file-path adjustments.

## Security and Privacy

- MyCloud works with a local folder you choose instead of creating a separate MyCloud storage account.
- The dashboard requires an `APP_PASSWORD` before files can be viewed, uploaded, downloaded, or converted.
- Syncthing synchronization happens only with devices that you explicitly pair and authorize.
- Do not forward port `5000` through your router.
- Use Tailscale Serve for private remote access from trusted devices.
- Tailscale Serve is private to your Tailscale network; do not use Tailscale Funnel if you do not want public internet access.
- Keep your Syncthing API key, MyCloud password, Flask secret key, and private configuration values out of GitHub.
- Install Tailscale only on devices and accounts you trust.
- Choose a strong, unique MyCloud password.

